Commonly used detectors in experimental stations include X-ray film, ionization chamber and X-ray sCMOS detector.
1) X-ray film
The experimental station is equipped with FUJI-IX50 and IX80 industrial X-ray films. The diameter of the film latex particles before development is about 0.3~0.8m. It is used with X-ray development and fixer and is mainly used for white light topography of single crystal materials.
2) Ionization chamber
The ionization chamber used in the experimental station is an Ar gas-filled ionization chamber, which is often used to measure the relative light intensity of X-rays when dimming or scanning the crystal rocking curve.
• When using, ensure that X-rays are incident on the window of the ionization chamber;
• Apply a certain bias voltage (400V) to the two plates of the ionization chamber;
• The photocurrent signal measured in the ionization chamber is amplified by a microcurrent meter (KEITHLEY 6517) and transmitted to the computer for data collection and display;
3) X-ray sCMOS detector
The commonly used detectors at the experimental station are a set of fiber-coupled sCMOS imaging detectors C12849 from Hamamatsu, Japan, and a set of self-developed lens-coupled imaging detectors with switchable magnifications. The lens-coupled detector can switch 1X, 2X, 4X, and 10X lenses. Hamamatsu Flash4.0 sCMOS and Hamamatsu C15550 qCMOS detectors are commonly used for image signal collection.

white light topography
X-ray topography is a method to examine the surface and internal microstructure defects of crystal materials and devices based on the changes in diffraction contrast and image elimination rules in the crystal. It is widely used in the study of the integrity of crystal materials. The white light morphology experiment uses the Laue diffraction method. The incident X-rays are continuous broad-spectrum X-rays. Each Laue spot obtained represents the structural information of the corresponding diffraction surface. The key points in the white light morphology experiment are:
• The research object of the white light morphology experiment is single crystal materials;
• The white-light X-ray interface size provided by the experimental station is up to 25×14mm2, which can be applied to the research of larger-sized samples;
• In white light morphology experiments, a precision four-knife slit can be used to adjust the incident light spot to a suitable size. For some samples with large scattering, the size of the incident light spot should be smaller than the size of the sample.
• For direct X-rays transmitted through the sample, lead sheets must be pasted on the corresponding parts of the film box to block them;
• White light morphology experiments use X-ray film to capture Laue diffraction spots, and the exposure time of the film is generally a few seconds;
• In order to prevent the influence of scattered light on imaging, lead sheets can be used to block stray light in white light morphology experiments;
• When preparing samples for white light morphology experiments, the residual stress inside the samples should be reduced as much as possible;
• After obtaining the results of the white light morphology experiment, the Laue diffraction spots must be indexed to determine the crystal structure information represented by each diffraction spot.

4B7B Beamline General Operations (required reading for users)
On and off light operation
Turning on the light refers to letting synchrotron radiation X-rays enter the measurement room. The steps to open the beam line are as follows:
1. Turn on all the gate valve control switches except the leftmost switch on the gate valve control box (a green indicator light on the switch is on);
2. Open the "safety shutter" (press the green button, the red button light goes out, and the green button light turns on);
3. Open the "photon shutter" (press the green button, the red button light goes out, and the green button light turns on);
Turning off the light means stopping synchrotron radiation X-rays from entering the measurement room. The steps to turn off the beam line are as follows:
1. Close the "safety shutter" (press the red button, the green button light goes out, and the red button light turns on)
2. Close the "photon shutter" (press the red button, the green button light goes out, and the red button light turns on)
3. Close the second and third switches from the left on the gate valve control box;
Precautions for turning on and off the light:
1. The "safety shutter" should not be opened and closed frequently. It should be closed when the user does not use the beam line for a long time (such as eating, storage ring failure, etc. for more than half an hour).
2. Operate accurately and do not touch other switches

